  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 AM MDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nIt was a typical monsoon day yesterday, with some areas, mainly in \nsouthwest and in Johnson and Natrona Counties getting a LOT of rain, \ncausing some local flooding. Other areas saw nothing. And we \ncontinue to have some showers and embedded thunder around late \ntonight, although intensity is significantly less. And these will \ncontinue through the night, gradually becoming weaker and less \nnumerous.\n\nWe will have another round of showers and storms today. There \nare some differences. For one, precipitable water values are \nless today by about 20 percent in most locations. This should \nbring less coverage and somewhat lesser rainfall rates. The \nother difference is that there will be more steering flow today,\ngenerally about 10 to 15 knots at 700 millibars and 25 to 35 \nknots at 500 millibars. So storms should have more movement. \nThis should decrease the chance of flooding somewhat. We still \nhave an excessive rainfall outlook across most ares west of the\nDivide though, so the threat is still there. There is also the \nrisk of strong to severe thunderstorms. Most of the area is \nunder a Marginal risk of severe weather today. There could be \nsome small hail, but this not the main risk. The main risk looks\nto be strong wind gusts. Roughly north and west of a Buffalo to\nRiverton to Lyman line has a Conditional 1 outlook for wind, or\npossible convective wind gusts to 75 mph. Soundings do show \nsome inverted-V profiles, especially across northwestern \nWyoming where the surface will be drier, with low and mid level \nlapse rates close to 9 C/km. This could lead to some strong \ndownburst winds later today. Again, the best chance would be \nacross the northwest but most areas will have a chance. And, \nlike previous nights, most convection should end after sunset \nbut a few showers and storms will likely linger through the \nnight. As for temperatures, they should be a few degrees warmer \nthan yesterday with more sunshine expected. \n\nThe rest of the week looks like more of the same as the annoying \nmonsoonal moisture will hang around the area through at least \nThursday and possibly into Friday as well. The models continue to \nhave different ideas on which day will be more active with no real \nwell defined shortwaves with changes from day to day. Precipitable \nwater generally average about 125 to 150 percent of normal through \nthe period though. So, at this point we went with a generic solution \nof mainly diurnal showers and storms each afternoon. Temperatures \nshould remain fairly similar through the period, not very hot but \naveraging around 4 to 8 degrees above normal on average, with the \npossibility of a cooler day if there is more cloud cover than \nexpected. Drier air may then work in for next weekend and bring a \nreturn to drier weather but uncertainty remains high on the exact \ntiming. So, all in all, it continues to look like more of the same \nas the summer of the monsoon lives on in western and central \nWyoming.",
